00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:13.360
So what is SRA?

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:15.960
It's part of NCBI,
which is part of NIH,

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:20.320
and it's the primary archive
of unassembled reads.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:22.320
So it doesn't have
final assemblies.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:24.000
It doesn't have
reference sequence.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.600
What it has is reads.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:28.320
So things that come off
the instruments.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.680
And that distinction
is worth keeping in mind.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:36.600
This is where you go when you
want access to the source data

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:38.680
for reference read --

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:41.480
Yeah, for reference sequence,
okay?

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:42.600
Things that are in GenBank.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:45.960
This is where you find
the evidence behind it.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:48.000
So it's a great place to get,
you know, I just said that.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:51.400
