00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:37.280
So let's talk about Galaxy.

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:39.840
Again, rough introduction before
we dive in

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:41.480
to the actual hands-on part.

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:45.840
Galaxy is a data integration
and analysis platform.

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:49.600
And it's funded for and
developed for life scientists.

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:52.960
It's actually used
in all sorts of things.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:55.840
But that's our goal,
is to be for life science.

00:07:55.840 --> 00:07:58.600
We are, in addition
to being that platform,

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:00.200
we're a worldwide community

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:01.840
and we really can't stress
that enough.

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:05.800
The community is what makes
Galaxy what it is.

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:08.160
And it's not just the coders,
the developers.

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:11.080
It's the users,
people who do training,

00:08:11.080 --> 00:08:13.800
people who provide
infrastructure.

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:17.640
It's a huge, very vibrant
community and without it,

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:21.000
Galaxy would probably not
be around anymore.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:23.600
So...Yeah.

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:25.000
So I like to stress
both those things.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:25.960
They're equally important --

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:28.840
the platform and the people.

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:33.480
Galaxy's available in a whole
bunch of different ways.

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:36.720
There are over 100 free,
online web servers out there.

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:41.400
Something like 140, 130,
the last I knew.

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:43.600
If you follow that first URL,
at the bottom,

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:45.720
you'll go to
the platform directory.

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:48.200
It's also available on
commercial and academic clouds

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:50.600
so you can easily
watch it on Amazon

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:52.400
or if you're in the U.S.,

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:55.200
you can easily watch it
on jet stream.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:57.560
People have published it
in containers

00:08:57.560 --> 00:08:58.800
and virtual machines

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:02.840
so you can launch it
on your laptop very easily.

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:05.600
It's also open source software
that can be installed anywhere.

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:08.920
And that is the most common
use case for Galaxy.

00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:11.800
It's installed all over
the world at research

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:15.600
institutions, universities,
usually behind firewalls.

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:18.200
But that is our most
popular deployment.

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:21.440
So...Okay.

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:23.720
We also have
a lot of training materials

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:27.960
and we run a tutorial
to highlight how to use SRA.

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:31.920
We'll use that later today.
But there is a huge library.

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:33.640
Again, this is part
of a community effort.

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:36.720
This is entirely run by the
community

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:38.400
to publish tutorials

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:41.520
on how to use Galaxy
for different domains

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:45.200
and all sorts of topics there --
epigenetics, genome annotation.

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:47.680
We also
have tutorials on Galaxy basics

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:50.440
like data manipulation,
user interface.

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:53.920
In addition, we have tutorials
on how to write tutorials,

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:56.040
which was very useful
for the three of us

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:57.840
in the last few days.

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:01.400
And we also have tutorials
on how to run a Galaxy server.

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:06.120
So it's a very robust library
of training methods

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:11.760
and something like 160 people
have contributed so far to that.

00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:15.960
So today, we're gonna do
a live demo.

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:19.760
We're gonna use
a COVID-19 example.

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:22.360
But that domain
doesn't actually matter.

00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:24.680
What we're demonstrating today
is how to use SRA

00:10:24.680 --> 00:10:27.800
and doesn't have to be viral
reads you're gathering from SRA,

00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:29.680
it can be anything.

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:31.720
It can back RNA-seq experiments,

00:10:31.720 --> 00:10:34.040
it can back ChIP-seq
experiments.

00:10:34.040 --> 00:10:35.720
You name it.

00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:38.080
If it's in SRA,
we can get it into Galaxy

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:40.120
with what we're going
to show you today.

