00:11:26.680 --> 00:11:32.680
So a couple of caveats
before we do this.

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:37.200
So SRA is this wonderful
resource chock-full of data.

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:39.880
And it's growing rapidly,
as you may know,

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:41.400
but submitters don't often

00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:43.480
provide complete
or correct metadata.

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:47.040
And we may see that in some
of our exploration today.

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:53.120
There's a discrepancy
between SRR and ERR entries.

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:55.520
Does anybody want to expand
on what that is?

00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:00.280
Van den Beek: Yes.
So actually, I don't know,

00:12:00.280 --> 00:12:03.800
maybe Yuriy can correct me,
but some thing --

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:09.080
Well, there are several places
where primary data

00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:10.800
is deposited.

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:14.400
So there is a shorter archive
here at NCBI.

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:19.640
There is also Short Read Archive
in European under EMBL umbrella.

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:22.480
European Nucleotide Archive.

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:27.160
And so sometimes, especially
for COVID, for SARS-CoV-2 data,

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:30.880
because it's it's released
essentially on a daily basis,

00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:32.520
They're not always in sync.

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:34.560
So, for example,
if you're trying

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:38.600
to pull ERR European extensions
from SRA from here,

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:40.400
sometimes that does not work.

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:43.000
So there is
some discrepancy here

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:45.600
and also in some cases
when you are trying to download

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:51.680
really large sets
of reads, thousands of reads,

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:55.160
then some of them
may statistically fail

00:12:55.160 --> 00:12:57.560
for a variety of reasons.

00:12:57.560 --> 00:13:02.640
Most often for network
connectivity reasons.

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:04.040
Clements: So the tool
we're going to use today

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:06.560
is actually built
to deal with that.

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:08.760
We won't -- I'm pretty sure
