and somebody from our team
will respond.

00:58:29.120 --> 00:58:31.560
And that's, you know,
"What went wrong?"

00:58:31.560 --> 00:58:33.440
So you could say, "Hello.

00:58:33.440 --> 00:58:38.120
I tried to get assist,

00:58:38.120 --> 00:58:44.560
but I don't know
what I did wrong."

00:58:48.720 --> 00:58:51.560
So we receive this report
and look at this.

00:58:51.560 --> 00:58:55.600
We try to help you
however we can.

00:58:55.600 --> 00:59:00.320
It also already says here
that there was an exception.

00:59:00.320 --> 00:59:02.920
So you see here.

00:59:02.920 --> 00:59:08.000
[ Speaks indistinctly ]

00:59:08.000 --> 00:59:10.360
So we actually want to help.

00:59:10.360 --> 00:59:12.840
And in fact,
we can use the rewind button.

00:59:12.840 --> 00:59:15.440
This is a very cool feature
in Galaxy.

00:59:15.440 --> 00:59:17.160
And so that this was
all part of a database

00:59:17.160 --> 00:59:18.760
for disability reasons.

00:59:18.760 --> 00:59:20.520
So if something goes wrong

00:59:20.520 --> 00:59:22.480
or you want
to change a parameter,

00:59:22.480 --> 00:59:26.080
you can hit this
rewind button.

00:59:26.080 --> 00:59:33.240
And so all we need to do here
is change H1 for Chrome.

00:59:33.240 --> 00:59:36.280
And then we can run again.

00:59:41.120 --> 00:59:44.400
[ Speaks indistinctly ]

00:59:48.600 --> 00:59:49.960
Alright.

00:59:49.960 --> 00:59:51.840
So back to what you said.

00:59:51.840 --> 00:59:55.240
So we have a fastq report.

00:59:55.240 --> 00:59:59.560
And there is only one data
set that is compatible,

00:59:59.560 --> 01:00:02.120
so it's very easy
to select this.

01:00:04.640 --> 01:00:10.160
We also have a report
and some stats.

01:00:13.720 --> 01:00:17.560
So this is this data set here.

01:00:17.560 --> 01:00:23.760
And...we have...

01:00:23.760 --> 01:00:26.280
snip and report.

01:00:26.280 --> 01:00:30.040
[ Speaks indistinctly ]

01:00:39.160 --> 01:00:43.080
Okay. So now,
what did you see this morning?

01:00:43.080 --> 01:00:44.600
We can have a look
at these tables,

01:00:44.600 --> 01:00:49.960
so this is considerably
more friendly than it was.

01:00:49.960 --> 01:00:52.320
So this is a regular table
that you could download

01:00:52.320 --> 01:00:55.200
and put in a spreadsheet,
editorial,

01:00:55.200 --> 01:00:57.320
or you can have a look here.

01:00:59.280 --> 01:01:02.200
So...

01:01:02.200 --> 01:01:04.400
See here which data set this is,

01:01:04.400 --> 01:01:06.160
and I'm always hoping
just the first one,

01:01:06.160 --> 01:01:12.400
you can see that there is
a varying number of...

01:01:12.400 --> 01:01:14.960
number of lines in the data set,

01:01:14.960 --> 01:01:18.840
and this corresponds
to the number of variants

01:01:18.840 --> 01:01:23.920
that it found,
you see here the impact, so...

01:01:25.640 --> 01:01:29.920
For instance, here are
some low-impact mutations,

01:01:29.920 --> 01:01:33.600
variants where we've
been taking sun,

01:01:33.600 --> 01:01:37.320
and they have a lot of mutation

01:01:37.320 --> 01:01:40.000
or we have some things
that are most severe.

01:01:40.000 --> 01:01:43.800
Typically the most severe things
have lower frequencies.

01:01:43.800 --> 01:01:46.520
In the sample,
they were quite rare.

01:01:49.040 --> 01:01:50.600
Okay.

01:01:50.600 --> 01:01:55.760
[ Speaks indistinctly ]

01:01:55.760 --> 01:01:59.920
So it gives us a global overview
of what we've done

01:01:59.920 --> 01:02:05.240
and we can begin
to take anything into space.

01:02:05.240 --> 01:02:09.920
And so these are
some general statistics.

01:02:09.920 --> 01:02:12.800
You can see various things here.

01:02:12.800 --> 01:02:18.600
So, for instance, one data set
at a high level of duplication.

01:02:18.600 --> 01:02:22.800
So perhaps it was sequenced
very deep.

01:02:22.800 --> 01:02:25.400
We can also see that
in that percent,

01:02:25.400 --> 01:02:26.960
it's going to be low.

01:02:26.960 --> 01:02:29.240
So this might indicate that
maybe there is some human needs

01:02:29.240 --> 01:02:31.520
that are within this mixture.

01:02:31.520 --> 01:02:33.760
There's few proper pairs,
which again,

01:02:33.760 --> 01:02:38.600
highlight what have been
artificially moved.

01:02:40.920 --> 01:02:45.600
And we can see...

01:02:45.600 --> 01:02:47.600
where...

01:02:50.240 --> 01:02:53.760
We can see the impact
that it had.

01:02:57.040 --> 01:03:01.600
[ Speaks indistinctly ]

01:03:01.600 --> 01:03:04.800
So let's keep in mind
this is a list.

01:03:04.800 --> 01:03:08.400
Typically, you do
some addition of it.

01:03:11.040 --> 01:03:14.360
Okay, you can see
how many reads we have,

01:03:14.360 --> 01:03:16.800
so this is the largest data set.

01:03:16.800 --> 01:03:19.120
This is the smallest data set.

01:03:23.320 --> 01:03:25.840
Okay.

