answered in the Q&amp;A.

01:03:59.360 --> 01:04:01.960
We currently have one
open question from Pablo.

01:04:01.960 --> 01:04:03.280
"In the trimming step,

01:04:03.280 --> 01:04:05.640
does Galaxy identify
automatically all sorts

01:04:05.640 --> 01:04:07.440
of adapter sequences
that may be present

01:04:07.440 --> 01:04:11.000
or should we specify them?"

01:04:11.000 --> 01:04:12.120
If you know them,

01:04:12.120 --> 01:04:14.960
it's always better to specify
them explicitly.

01:04:14.960 --> 01:04:16.400
If you don't know them,

01:04:16.400 --> 01:04:21.000
it does recognize the most
common sequence and adapters,

01:04:21.000 --> 01:04:24.080
but if their prevalence
is relatively low,

01:04:24.080 --> 01:04:25.800
it might not find them.

01:04:25.800 --> 01:04:28.680
So that's...

01:04:28.680 --> 01:04:30.400
I just want to add here

01:04:30.400 --> 01:04:32.680
that Galaxy
doesn't identify anything.

01:04:32.680 --> 01:04:35.440
What it identifies
is the tools that it has.

01:04:35.440 --> 01:04:39.200
So in this particular case,
we're using fastp,

01:04:39.200 --> 01:04:42.040
which recognizes
automatically some types

01:04:42.040 --> 01:04:44.400
of adapters and trims them.

01:04:44.400 --> 01:04:46.320
There is also trimmomatic,
which is in the Galaxy,

01:04:46.320 --> 01:04:47.920
which we didn't use
in this example.

01:04:47.920 --> 01:04:49.840
There is Trim Galore!

01:04:49.840 --> 01:04:52.240
In particular, in case
of SARS-CoV-2,

01:04:52.240 --> 01:04:54.200
if you're using ampliconic data,

01:04:54.200 --> 01:04:57.680
so this data sets
have specific primers

01:04:57.680 --> 01:05:00.200
that they're used for amplifying
in reaching,

01:05:00.200 --> 01:05:02.320
particularly SARS-CoV-2
sequences.

01:05:02.320 --> 01:05:04.800
And so you have to know them

01:05:04.800 --> 01:05:07.560
and there are tools for dealing
with them specifically.

01:05:07.560 --> 01:05:13.480
That is an IVAR tool set
for cleaving of amplicon primers

01:05:13.480 --> 01:05:14.640
off of these reads.

01:05:14.640 --> 01:05:15.840
It's also in Galaxy.

01:05:15.840 --> 01:05:17.800
We're not showing it
as part of this example,

01:05:17.800 --> 01:05:18.880
but it's there.

01:05:18.880 --> 01:05:20.200
But in order to use it,

01:05:20.200 --> 01:05:23.840
you need to know adapter
sequences that you're using.

01:05:25.040 --> 01:05:26.840
Thanks, Anton.

01:05:29.040 --> 01:05:33.480
Another question
from Tusharica.

01:05:33.480 --> 01:05:36.000
"Is it possible to form plots
in Galaxy which show us

01:05:36.000 --> 01:05:39.560
which are the common genes
present in each sample?"

01:05:44.280 --> 01:05:48.040
Anybody want to take that?

01:05:48.040 --> 01:05:51.440
In relation to SARS-CoV-2
or in general?

01:05:51.440 --> 01:05:53.920
I'm not sure.

01:05:53.920 --> 01:05:58.200
I doubt any of us understands
exactly what do you mean.

01:05:58.200 --> 01:06:02.800
So in general.
-In general.

01:06:02.800 --> 01:06:07.680
Okay, so, I mean,

01:06:07.680 --> 01:06:13.400
this is a bit difficult
to answer that

01:06:13.400 --> 01:06:15.240
from multiple perspectives.

01:06:15.240 --> 01:06:17.760
So if you want to see,
for instance,

01:06:17.760 --> 01:06:20.400
which genes have a mutation,

01:06:20.400 --> 01:06:22.040
we could generate
a tabular file,

01:06:22.040 --> 01:06:24.360
and then there are tools
in Galaxy

01:06:24.360 --> 01:06:27.440
to plot tabular data.

01:06:27.440 --> 01:06:31.920
There are visualizations
we can display.

01:06:31.920 --> 01:06:34.760
One axis versus the other.

01:06:34.760 --> 01:06:37.400
These are relatively
simple tools.

01:06:37.400 --> 01:06:41.400
If you do know how to use
ART Studio or Jupiter,

01:06:41.400 --> 01:06:44.160
you can load up the data
that are generated

01:06:44.160 --> 01:06:47.680
and then use ART Studio
or Jupiter within Galaxy

01:06:47.680 --> 01:06:50.800
to look at that question.

01:06:50.800 --> 01:06:53.320
And we have also --

01:06:53.320 --> 01:06:55.760
we have a GT plot,

01:06:55.760 --> 01:06:59.040
which also lets you display
different columns,

01:06:59.040 --> 01:07:00.240
different colors.

01:07:00.240 --> 01:07:02.840
So we don't have
necessarily something

01:07:02.840 --> 01:07:04.360
specifically for genes,

01:07:04.360 --> 01:07:06.400
but as soon as we put that
into a table,

01:07:06.400 --> 01:07:09.120
you can generate plots.

